# Monte Carlo estimation of π: error scaling

For each sample size N, we drew N independent uniform points (x,y) in [0,1)² and estimated π as 4K/N, where K counts points with x²+y² ≤ 1. We ran 50 estimates at each N using `numpy.random.default_rng(2026+i)`, i = 0,…,49, drawing an (N,2) array for each run. The generator was restarted for every (N,i): runs are independent within each N, while reuse of seeds pairs the estimates across sample sizes. We calculated MAE = (1/50) Σᵢ |π̂ᵢ − `math.pi`|.

| Samples, N | Mean absolute error |
| ---: | ---: |
| 1,000 | 0.0442711 |
| 10,000 | 0.0131615 |
| 100,000 | 0.00453848 |

Mean absolute error of the quarter-circle Monte Carlo estimate of π relative to `math.pi`, averaged over 50 independent runs at each N. Errors are dimensionless and shown to six significant digits.

Unweighted least squares with an intercept, using the three unrounded MAEs, gave

ln(MAE) = 0.274464 − 0.494605 ln(N).

The fitted exponent **−0.494605** is close to the expected **−1/2**, differing by +0.005395. For K ∼ Binomial(N, π/4), Var(4K/N) = π(4−π)/N; the normal approximation therefore predicts mean absolute error proportional to N^(−1/2). The measured decrease is consistent with this rate over the tested range. With only three sample sizes and 50 runs per size, this is a finite-sample comparison, not proof of the asymptotic law.
